---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, DEFAULT_QUOTA, GITHUB_URL, SIGNUP_CURL, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="What nano-api is, who wrote it, why it is free, and the limits that exist anyway."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>Small things I kept rebuilding, and no plan to sell you anything.</h1>

				<p>
					Every project I have worked on needed some of the same small pieces. Something to notice
					when a nightly job stops running. Something to call a URL on a schedule, in the right
					timezone. A lock, so two instances do not both do the work. One value I can change
					without a deploy. A counter. None of them is hard. All of them are fiddly, and I have
					written each one more than once, in a hurry, slightly wrong.
				</p>
				<p>
					So I wrote them once more, carefully, and put them behind an HTTP endpoint on
					infrastructure that does not need me awake. They are here. Take a key and use them.
				</p>
				<pre class="signup"><code>{SIGNUP_CURL}</code></pre>

				<h2>Why it is free</h2>
				<p>
					Because charging would be a promise. Taking money means promising a response time and an
					uptime I cannot honestly promise alongside a day job — and I would rather hand you
					something useful with clear limits than something priced with vague ones. The
					infrastructure costs a few dollars a month whether anyone uses it or not.
				</p>
				<p>
					If that ever changes you will get an email well before anything costs money, nothing
					will be switched off without you agreeing first, and nothing you built will break: the
					API does not change shape because billing does.
				</p>

				<h2>How it is built</h2>
				<p>
					Cloudflare Workers at the edge, D1 for storage, one scheduled trigger every minute for
					the background work. No servers, no containers, no queue to babysit — which is the only
					reason a free tier can exist at all.
				</p>
				<p>
					API keys are stored as SHA-256 hashes, never in clear text. You rotate and revoke them
					yourself, without asking me. The whole thing is
					<a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">on GitHub</a> if you want to see how
					any of it works, or lift a piece of it for your own use.
				</p>

				<h2>Limits that exist anyway</h2>
				<p>These are technical, not commercial — they stop one account taking the service down for everyone.</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						{DEFAULT_QUOTA} monitors, schedules, configs or counters by default. Ask and it goes
						up; that is a one-line change and it costs nothing.
					</li>
					<li>
						The quota applies to <em>creating</em> things. Pings and scheduled runs for things
						that already exist are never refused for quota reasons.
					</li>
					<li>Ping payloads 2 KB; scheduled request bodies 8 KB.</li>
					<li>Scheduled calls: 60s timeout, 3 attempts, public HTTPS targets only.</li>
					<li>100 distinct lock names, lease up to an hour.</li>
					<li>Three signups per address per hour, which keeps the front door honest.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Things worth knowing before you rely on it</h2>

				<div class="qa">
					<h3>How fast will I know something broke?</h3>
					<p>
						A sweep runs every minute, so an alert lands within about a minute of the deadline. If
						you need second-level precision, that is a different product and I would say so.
					</p>

					<h3>Where do alerts go?</h3>
					<p>
						Any HTTPS webhook. The body is Slack-shaped (<code>text</code> at the top level), so a
						Slack webhook needs no configuration at all. Email and SMS are not built.
					</p>

					<h3>Is there a dashboard?</h3>
					<p>
						No, and not by accident. There is an API and
						<a href="/howto/">a page that walks through all of it in curl</a>. A dashboard would be
						the next thing I build badly.
					</p>

					<h3>What if the service itself goes down?</h3>
					<p>
						Then you are not monitored, and there is no SLA to point at. I will not promise one I
						cannot keep. Where a missed alert would genuinely hurt, run two providers — I would
						tell a friend the same thing.
					</p>

					<h3>Do you look at my data?</h3>
					<p>
						No. Delete a monitor and it is gone, history included. There is no analytics script on
						this site either.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2>Who</h2>
				<p>
					One person, in Norway, evenings and weekends. If something is broken, missing, or just
					wrong-headed, you reach the person who wrote the code, because there is only the one:
					<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or
					<a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 46em;
		padding: 2em 0 0;
	}
	.page h2 {
		margin-top: 2.2em;
		font-size: 1.45rem;
	}
	.page p,
	.page li {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.signup {
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0;
	}
	.page ul {
		padding-left: 1.2em;
	}
	.page li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
	.qa h3 {
		margin-top: 1.8em;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--text);
	}
</style>
